\label{thm_equiv_forms_of_max_idempot_filter}
    Let $\mathscr{F}$ be a family.  The following are equivalent:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item
        \label{item_max_prop_idemp_filter}
        the family $\mathscr{F}$ is a proper, idempotent filter and is maximal (by containment) in the set of proper, idempotent filters;

        \item
        \label{item_ultra_idemp}
        the family $\mathscr{F}$ is an ultrafilter that satisfies $\mathscr{F} \subseteq \mathscr{F} + \mathscr{F}$;

        \item
        \label{item_ultra_strong_idemp}
        the family $\mathscr{F}$ is an ultrafilter that satisfies $\mathscr{F} = \mathscr{F} + \mathscr{F}$.
    \end{enumerate}
    In particular, every proper, idempotent filter is contained in an idempotent ultrafilter.

\label{rmk_idempotents_without_zorn}
    A routine application of Zorn's lemma gives the existence of maximal proper, idempotent filters.  It follows from \cref{thm_equiv_forms_of_max_idempot_filter} that these are ultrafilters.  The proof given here relies on the fact that a compact, Hausdorff, right-topological semigroup contains an idempotent element (see \cite[Thm. 2.5]{hindman_strauss_book_2012} and the end-of-chapter notes). A short, topology-free proof of this was given by Papazyan \cite{papazyan_1989}, and a proof without the full strength of Zorn's Lemma was given by Di Nasso and Tachtsis \cite{dinasso_tachtsis_2018}.

    Another routine application of Zorn's lemma gives the existence of maximal proper, translation-invariant filters.  It is an easy exercise to show that there are no translation-invariant ultrafilters on $\mathbb{N}$.  Therefore, in contrast to maximal proper, idempotent filters, maximal proper, translation-invariant filters are not ultrafilters.

